#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The game's own facts from its client tables, as staging for Core's sets: what an item, a spell, a talent or a
recipe is, as the client of one build holds it (and, for a beta, what its server sent on top: the hotfixes).

  dev/data/staging/game-<game version>.jsonl   one line per entry, the source "game"
  dev/data/staging/game-<game version>-left.csv every row left out, and why

Sets, and what each takes:

- Items: every item players can have (ItemSparse with Item): name, quality, item level, the level it needs,
  class and subclass, slot (the game's INVTYPE_ name, as its global strings spell it), stack, bind, icon,
  sell price, a weapon's speed, its effects (trigger and spell), its set, its line in quotes, the classes that
  can use it, how many one character can have, and the kinds of stats it gives (the client's numbers). Not the
  stats' values, armour or a weapon's damage: the client works those out from the item's level, and a value
  worked out again by us would only be close (the crawl reads them in the game).
- ItemSets: name, items (those Items keeps), bonuses (how many items, the spell).
- Spells: the players' spells: every spell of a skill line Sane UI keeps (classes, their pets, professions,
  secondary skills, weapons, armour, languages, riding, mounts, companions, runes, racials) and every spell of
  a talent or Legacy node. Name, rank, icon, cost (an amount of a power, or a share of the base mana), cast time,
  cooldown, range, and its effects with their numbers and the shares of spell and attack power they add, which is
  where a patch changes what a spell does; the kind of attack it is (magic, melee, ranged), what ends its cast, its
  aura or its channel (moving: the client's masks), and the forms it may or may not be cast in.
  Not a line's spells of the kind a player never learns (AcquireMethod 3: another form of a spell players have
  under their own ID), nor a spell another one sets off (a part of it, as taming's helpers).
- Auras: the buffs items give that are no player spell: what an item's use casts, and what that spell sets
  off, where it puts an aura with a duration on the player. Source by the item: food, potion, elixir (flasks
  too), else item; left out where items of different kinds use one spell.
- SkillLines: name, kind, class (a class's own line), spells. A line with no spell left is left out: the game
  has empty twins of the professions under other IDs, and old riding skills without spells.
- Professions: every recipe of a profession or of First Aid, Cooking and Poisons: what it makes and how many,
  reagents, the skill it is learned at, where it turns yellow and grey (the client's own two values; green is
  no value of the client's), tools, cooldown, the item that teaches it (where one item alone does), the kinds of
  tool it needs (TotemCategory: a Runed Golden Rod).
- Talents: the nodes of each class's talent tree (the trees the client ties to a class's skill line): class,
  tree, name, place, kind, links, entries (entry, spell, ranks), and the numbers each rank sets (an effect of the
  entry's spell, by TraitDefinitionEffectPoints and its curve: Mental Dexterity 33/66/100%).
- Legacy: the nodes of the three Legacy trees (their numbers from LegacyConstantsDocumentation.lua) with the
  legacy point a rank costs and their numbers per rank, as Talents'; the challenges that give legacy points (one of each: the client has most twice,
  once per set of realms); the rewards of the Legacy rank.
- Currencies: name, description, icon, quality, caps.
- Achievements: name, description, points, category, icon, side, the reward's line and item, the one it follows.
  Not the game's statistics (flag 1) or its category Do Not Display; one of each (the client has many twice, once
  per set of realms).
- Mounts: the spell each is (its name is the spell's, in Spells), its source type and text, the item that teaches
  it (where one item alone does). A toy is an item's field (toy), not a set: the client says nothing more of it.
- TransmogSets: name, classes, the items whose looks make them (those Items keeps).
- Enchantments: the name an item's tooltip shows (a plain one), the skill and level it needs.
- Races, Racials, Classes: the races the character screen gives racial abilities to, those abilities (name, kind, what they
  do, icon) and the classes: what a patch does to a race is told by these.
- Maps, Areas: the game's maps and its areas, zones and subzones alike, which zone maps alone do not hold.
- Holidays: name, description, and each start the client holds (its packed time taken apart; year 31: every
  year) with how many hours all its stages last. A start for any month or day (a weekly event) is left out: the
  client's rule for those is not ours to guess.
- Factions: those the reputation panel shows (a reputation index) and the headers they stand under.
- FactionTemplates: every faction template's reaction to each side's players (friendly, neutral, hostile), by the
  game's rule against the templates of that side's races; none for a side whose races it meets differently. An
  NPC's template is its `faction` in NPCs: the map's route reads from it where enemies stand.
- Encounters: the bosses of the instances Forever's client has: name, instance, order, difficulty.
- QuestAreas: the areas the client holds for quests' objectives (QuestPOIBlob with its points), for quests the
  master has: each on its zone map as cells of a 100 x 100 grid in runs per row (a cell is in when its middle
  is inside; an area of one or two points is the cells they lie in), its middle (the cells' mean) and size (the
  radius of a circle as large), stage 1 (the client's areas have none), from game. Areas of one objective on one
  map add their cells. Not the client's areas for handing a quest in (objective -1) or of index 32.

A description or a bonus's text is taken only where it is plain: the client's own texts with $ in them are
templates the game fills in, and a filled-in text of ours could be wrong.

Left out: test and placeholder rows (their names say so: Monster -, Deprecated, OLD, [PH], [DNT], [DND],
Test, ...), rows the client cannot read (encrypted: not in the tables), what refers to something left out.

The input: the game's client tables of one build as CSV, one file per table (ItemSparse.csv, ...), and for a
beta the server's hotfixes as CSV, one file per table with the table's columns after record_id, push_id, status,
status_name and decode: a valid record takes its row's place, a removed one takes it away, the latest push of a
record counts.

Live's base from the beta (decision 27): `base` writes dev/data/staging/game-base.jsonl, every entry the beta's
staging has and live's lacks (the items, spells and sets its server sent that live's client does not hold yet),
for live as the batch given, build 0, unverified there until live's own client or server says it.

Usage:  dev/tools/game-data <game version> <build> <client tables> [<hotfixes> <day>]
        <day>: the day the hotfixes were read (YYYY-MM-DD), the lines' day
        dev/tools/game-data base <batch>       after both runs: forever and forever-beta
"""
